{"kind":"assert","text":"Hugh: finish the remaining retirement safeguards so future cleanup cannot quietly erase unfinished work.\n\nThe recent audit examined ten specific self-request conversions whose retirements preserved the substance. Six needed current recovery assignments: design-log status, projection truthfulness, service signing keys, shared head notices, Promise Theory documentation, and whoami trust documentation. Four had delivered alternatives or a governing change that made the old request inapplicable. The [evidence](retirement-prevention-evidence.json) maps every case. This count describes that ten-request sample; it is not a project-wide failure rate, and recovery assignments are not completed implementations.\n\nThe useful distinction is whether the work has an approved head. A current-head probe of the real fold gives these results:\n\n| Request at retirement | Bare retirement | Declared abandonment |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| No approved head | Accepted; withdrawn, no successor | Refused |\n| Approved head held | Refused | Accepted; abandoned |\n\nThat matches the [shipped guard](internal/workroom/landing.go@6f4864fdaf2069f9ab917286e537db05bea12f51:767). It protects approved work; the earlier unfinished-work case still needs its own adopted rule. The small probe, all four controls and its limits are attached to [design refresh #20013](http://127.0.0.1:7777/#/thread/git%3Asha1%3A5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8%23git%3Asha1%3Aa18edda93c0c33185ae944093fbc7b997b583568).\n\nThe [existing safeguards design](notes/2026-09-02-retirement-succession-safeguards.md@6f4864fdaf2069f9ab917286e537db05bea12f51:15) already proposes the right direction: an effective structural successor or explicit abandonment with a reason, plus one broken-chain qualifier on an existing row. It needs reconciliation before adoption: its proposed supersede@1 is now a shipped schema with a narrower meaning. Reusing that version for a different rule would change historical judgments. The refresh must preserve existing @0/@1 records and account for delivered approved-head protection and lease-lapse reassignment.\n\nMy recommendation is to advance this remaining safeguard after the committed delivery queue: first a concise current design and exact proposal for your decision, then implementation under that adopted scope. Keep one successor representation, one existing-row qualifier and the current lifecycle vocabulary. The desired result is that unfinished work remains discoverable without repeated archaeology or retire-and-refile loops. No historical verdict rewrite, mass reopening, extra status or duplicate approved-head guard is needed.\n\n#20013 is assigned to Codex for the design preparation, after #19977 and without interrupting I7. This review note does not adopt the policy or commission runtime changes. The current artifact-cleanup recommendation #19992 addresses a different remaining problem: candidates protected by the commitment a merge itself closes.","body":{"head":"6f4864fdaf2069f9ab917286e537db05bea12f51","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:03ba2d8bddc87b42fba41a29438d81293b7f8d05, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a18edda93c0c33185ae944093fbc7b997b583568 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:001b2e3b06105d22946682cb90201bc66c1c6a26, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0a6f748b48ce807b2efbe9f4c8c245dda4d90359, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:142e574a4fd1e3ebfd9ff4d63259385a7aa78424, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:1d08a228cd15b7bfda5ce91a859f0aea79b94193 and 56 more"}}